WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.471 --> 00:00:04.387
In the previous video, we described a
distribution's centre.

2
00:00:04.467 --> 00:00:09.158
We saw that the mean alone may not be enough

3
00:00:09.238 --> 00:00:13.751
to characterise the data: we also need to
quantify their spread.

4
00:00:13.831 --> 00:00:16.733
For that, we'll introduce two more measures:

5
00:00:16.813 --> 00:00:19.775
standard deviation and variance.

6
00:00:19.855 --> 00:00:21.266
Let's take an example.

7
00:00:21.346 --> 00:00:24.507
Consider two farms or forestry operations

8
00:00:24.599 --> 00:00:26.874
measuring biomass production

9
00:00:26.954 --> 00:00:29.447
in tonnes per hectare, each across four plots.

10
00:00:29.527 --> 00:00:32.952
Both have the same mean of four tonnes per
hectare,

11
00:00:33.032 --> 00:00:36.839
but the situations are different. On the first

12
00:00:36.919 --> 00:00:40.837
farm, A, every plot produces the same amount.

13
00:00:40.917 --> 00:00:44.859
On the second, two plots produce much more

14
00:00:44.983 --> 00:00:47.690
and two produce much less.

15
00:00:47.770 --> 00:00:50.245
Let's now try to quantify

16
00:00:50.325 --> 00:00:52.916
the spread of the values on farm B.

17
00:00:52.996 --> 00:00:56.865
We'll take its values and calculate

18
00:00:56.945 --> 00:01:00.813
how far each one is from the mean.

19
00:01:00.893 --> 00:01:04.640
The first and second plots

20
00:01:04.720 --> 00:01:08.925
produce two tonnes per hectare less than the
mean;

21
00:01:09.005 --> 00:01:13.403
the third and fourth produce two tonnes per
hectare more.

22
00:01:14.041 --> 00:01:17.759
Now let's try to calculate the total
deviation.

23
00:01:17.839 --> 00:01:21.365
We could simply add these differences
together.

24
00:01:21.445 --> 00:01:24.635
Of course, we get zero: that's a property

25
00:01:24.715 --> 00:01:27.904
of the mean, the balance point of our series.

26
00:01:27.984 --> 00:01:31.799
Adding the deviations doesn't tell us how
widely

27
00:01:31.879 --> 00:01:35.581
the values are spread, because they balance
out.

28
00:01:36.057 --> 00:01:38.648
The problem here is the negative signs.

29
00:01:38.728 --> 00:01:40.806
We need to stop the signs cancelling out.

30
00:01:40.886 --> 00:01:42.502
How can we do that?

31
00:01:42.582 --> 00:01:45.944
One simple approach is to square them.

32
00:01:46.024 --> 00:01:48.769
Now all our values are positive:

33
00:01:48.849 --> 00:01:51.698
both (−2)² and (+2)² equal 4.

34
00:01:51.778 --> 00:01:53.701
We can then

35
00:01:53.781 --> 00:01:56.093
take the mean of these squared deviations.

36
00:01:56.185 --> 00:01:58.639
Since all four values are the same,

37
00:01:58.719 --> 00:02:01.845
namely 4, their mean is also 4.

38
00:02:02.681 --> 00:02:06.112
Previously, adding the deviations

39
00:02:06.192 --> 00:02:09.897
gave us zero. Now we have

40
00:02:09.977 --> 00:02:13.462
a non-zero value, showing that the second

41
00:02:13.542 --> 00:02:17.467
farm's observations are spread around the
mean.

42
00:02:17.547 --> 00:02:19.771
What we've just calculated

43
00:02:19.851 --> 00:02:22.623
is called the descriptive variance.

44
00:02:22.703 --> 00:02:24.214
So what is variance?

45
00:02:24.294 --> 00:02:27.421
It is the mean of the squared deviations

46
00:02:27.513 --> 00:02:29.940
from the mean of our data.

47
00:02:30.020 --> 00:02:33.789
The formula may look a little intimidating,

48
00:02:33.869 --> 00:02:38.571
but it describes exactly what we've just done.
Let's unpack it.

49
00:02:38.651 --> 00:02:41.486
Here, xᵢ stands for one observation.

50
00:02:41.566 --> 00:02:44.518
For example, x₁ is the first observation,

51
00:02:44.598 --> 00:02:47.842
x₂ is the second, and i takes each value

52
00:02:47.922 --> 00:02:51.421
from 1 up to the total number of plots.

53
00:02:51.513 --> 00:02:53.948
This i is called an index.

54
00:02:54.028 --> 00:02:57.920
When i is 1, we use x₁; when i is 2, we use
x₂, and so on.

55
00:02:58.000 --> 00:03:01.163
The symbol x̄, read 'x bar', is the mean of
our data.

56
00:03:01.243 --> 00:03:03.810
That's what we calculated earlier:

57
00:03:03.890 --> 00:03:06.869
4 for both farms.

58
00:03:07.449 --> 00:03:10.214
Here is our example again.

59
00:03:10.294 --> 00:03:13.983
For farm B, x₁ and x₂ are

60
00:03:14.063 --> 00:03:17.966
2, x₃ and x₄ are 6, and x̄ is

61
00:03:18.046 --> 00:03:22.740
the mean of all four values, shown by the bar.

62
00:03:23.206 --> 00:03:27.609
We calculate each observation's deviation

63
00:03:27.689 --> 00:03:32.466
from the mean, then square it.

64
00:03:32.546 --> 00:03:35.019
This symbol,

65
00:03:35.099 --> 00:03:37.946
capital sigma, which looks a bit like a
sideways M,

66
00:03:38.026 --> 00:03:41.184
tells us to add them:

67
00:03:41.264 --> 00:03:44.938
(x₁ − the mean)² + (x₂ − the mean)², and so
on.

68
00:03:45.030 --> 00:03:50.551
We start at x₁, hence i = 1, and continue to
xₙ,

69
00:03:50.631 --> 00:03:56.152
where n is the total number of observations in
our sample.

70
00:03:56.232 --> 00:03:59.139
Then we divide the whole sum

71
00:03:59.219 --> 00:04:02.425
by n to obtain the variance.

72
00:04:02.505 --> 00:04:07.210
It's exactly the process we've just followed.

73
00:04:07.462 --> 00:04:11.155
Let's work through the formula step by step.

74
00:04:11.235 --> 00:04:13.634
Here, n is 4 because we have four plots.

75
00:04:13.714 --> 00:04:16.491
We add (x₁ − x̄)², (x₂ − x̄)², and so on.

76
00:04:16.571 --> 00:04:19.887
Substituting the values for each plot

77
00:04:19.967 --> 00:04:23.390
gives 4, the variance we calculated earlier.

78
00:04:23.470 --> 00:04:26.116
This is the descriptive variance.

79
00:04:26.196 --> 00:04:28.682
A variance of zero, by contrast,

80
00:04:28.762 --> 00:04:31.996
means all the values are identical.

81
00:04:32.076 --> 00:04:35.978
Farm A had four values of four tonnes per
hectare,

82
00:04:36.070 --> 00:04:38.545
so its mean was 4 and its variance was zero.

83
00:04:38.625 --> 00:04:40.989
Farm B had exactly the same mean,

84
00:04:41.069 --> 00:04:43.822
but its variance is 4.

85
00:04:43.902 --> 00:04:46.099
So a variance of zero simply means

86
00:04:46.179 --> 00:04:48.901
that every observation has the same value.

87
00:04:49.360 --> 00:04:51.996
You may have noticed a slight difficulty

88
00:04:52.076 --> 00:04:54.763
with variance: its units.

89
00:04:54.843 --> 00:04:57.376
Because we squared the deviations,

90
00:04:57.456 --> 00:05:00.502
variance is measured in (tonnes per hectare)
squared,

91
00:05:00.582 --> 00:05:03.627
whereas biomass production was in tonnes per
hectare.

92
00:05:03.707 --> 00:05:06.702
That squared unit is harder to interpret.

93
00:05:06.782 --> 00:05:10.420
If I say the variance is 4 (tonnes per
hectare)²,

94
00:05:10.512 --> 00:05:12.298
it's not easy to picture what that means.

95
00:05:12.378 --> 00:05:15.489
That's why we introduce the standard
deviation,

96
00:05:15.569 --> 00:05:18.484
written with the Greek letter sigma. It's the
square root

97
00:05:18.564 --> 00:05:21.676
of the variance, which removes the square

98
00:05:21.756 --> 00:05:24.475
from the units. We then have

99
00:05:24.555 --> 00:05:28.305
a standard deviation in units that are easier
to interpret.

100
00:05:28.385 --> 00:05:31.447
A standard deviation of two tonnes per hectare
is

101
00:05:31.527 --> 00:05:34.964
more meaningful than a variance of 4 (tonnes
per hectare)².

102
00:05:35.056 --> 00:05:38.785
It describes farm B quite well here:

103
00:05:38.865 --> 00:05:42.695
the two lower values of 2 and the two higher
values

104
00:05:42.775 --> 00:05:46.707
of 6 are each two units away from the mean.

105
00:05:46.787 --> 00:05:49.601
That's why we introduced the standard
deviation.

106
00:05:49.681 --> 00:05:52.496
Intuitively, standard deviation and variance

107
00:05:52.576 --> 00:05:55.572
both describe the same underlying spread.

108
00:05:56.180 --> 00:05:59.819
Let's return to the normal distributions.

109
00:05:59.899 --> 00:06:03.766
Here, they have the same mean but different
standard deviations.

110
00:06:03.846 --> 00:06:07.599
These two curves have standard deviations of
0.7 and 1.5.

111
00:06:07.679 --> 00:06:10.540
A standard deviation of 1.5 gives a wider
distribution.

112
00:06:11.013 --> 00:06:14.753
Squaring deviations when calculating variance

113
00:06:14.833 --> 00:06:19.036
has another consequence: it amplifies large
deviations.

114
00:06:19.116 --> 00:06:22.625
For example, compare a deviation of 2

115
00:06:22.705 --> 00:06:26.503
with a deviation of 10. Their ratio is just 5.

116
00:06:26.583 --> 00:06:29.455
After squaring them,

117
00:06:29.535 --> 00:06:32.465
we get 4 and 100, whose ratio is 25.

118
00:06:32.545 --> 00:06:35.301
So squaring the deviations means

119
00:06:35.381 --> 00:06:38.217
that the larger a deviation is,

120
00:06:38.309 --> 00:06:42.331
the more strongly it affects the variance.

121
00:06:42.411 --> 00:06:45.749
Like the mean, both standard deviation and
variance

122
00:06:45.829 --> 00:06:49.224
are very sensitive to extreme values,

123
00:06:49.304 --> 00:06:52.756
in this example, unusually high ones.

124
00:06:52.836 --> 00:06:56.289
Changing the last value to 20

125
00:06:56.369 --> 00:06:59.707
increased the standard deviation almost
fivefold,

126
00:06:59.787 --> 00:07:03.433
while the mean increased much less.

127
00:07:03.525 --> 00:07:05.485
Keep that in mind

128
00:07:05.565 --> 00:07:07.572
when using variance or standard deviation.

129
00:07:07.652 --> 00:07:10.446
With an unusual value like 20, we should ask:

130
00:07:10.526 --> 00:07:13.136
'Is this a measurement error,

131
00:07:13.216 --> 00:07:16.196
or a real event that might be worth
investigating?'

132
00:07:16.276 --> 00:07:19.256
I'll keep coming back to this point.

133
00:07:19.336 --> 00:07:21.807
Make sure you understand where

134
00:07:21.887 --> 00:07:24.357
unusual observations come from before changing
them,

135
00:07:24.437 --> 00:07:27.497
including them or removing them from your
dataset.

136
00:07:28.357 --> 00:07:30.841
Here's a summary of how graphs

137
00:07:30.921 --> 00:07:33.832
can help us choose our summary measures.

138
00:07:33.912 --> 00:07:37.677
For a fairly symmetrical distribution without
major extremes,

139
00:07:37.757 --> 00:07:42.377
the mean and standard deviation, or variance,
can be useful.

140
00:07:42.457 --> 00:07:46.062
For a very asymmetrical distribution with

141
00:07:46.142 --> 00:07:49.694
some distant values, the median and another
measure

142
00:07:49.774 --> 00:07:53.513
we'll cover later, the interquartile range,

143
00:07:53.605 --> 00:07:57.252
may be more informative.

144
00:07:57.332 --> 00:08:00.932
Again, there's no magic rule for choosing
measures.

145
00:08:01.012 --> 00:08:04.281
But this gives you a sense of when one measure

146
00:08:04.361 --> 00:08:08.513
may be more useful than another, and where its
limits lie.

147
00:08:08.995 --> 00:08:11.994
Let's pause on an important detail:

148
00:08:12.074 --> 00:08:15.940
you may encounter two formulas for variance.

149
00:08:16.020 --> 00:08:18.250
One divides by n, the number

150
00:08:18.330 --> 00:08:21.185
of observations; the other divides by n − 1.

151
00:08:21.265 --> 00:08:23.013
Why the difference?

152
00:08:23.093 --> 00:08:26.622
We won't go into the mathematical details. The
aim is

153
00:08:26.702 --> 00:08:30.407
to understand the intuition and know when to
use each formula.

154
00:08:30.755 --> 00:08:34.600
If you want to describe the observations
actually present

155
00:08:34.680 --> 00:08:38.793
in your sample and table, you can divide by n.

156
00:08:38.873 --> 00:08:40.998
That's exactly what we did

157
00:08:41.078 --> 00:08:43.417
with our four plots: we divided by 4.

158
00:08:43.497 --> 00:08:47.288
If we use a suitable sample to estimate

159
00:08:47.368 --> 00:08:51.239
the variance of a larger population, we divide
by n − 1.

160
00:08:51.331 --> 00:08:55.257
The sample mean was itself

161
00:08:55.337 --> 00:08:59.685
calculated from the observations in that
sample.

162
00:08:59.765 --> 00:09:01.653
It's therefore particularly

163
00:09:01.733 --> 00:09:05.449
well positioned at the centre of those
observations.

164
00:09:05.529 --> 00:09:08.581
But it may be less representative

165
00:09:08.661 --> 00:09:13.003
of the population as a whole:

166
00:09:13.083 --> 00:09:16.811
it could sit a little below or above the
population mean.

167
00:09:16.891 --> 00:09:19.083
It fits the sample especially closely,

168
00:09:19.163 --> 00:09:21.927
rather than perfectly representing the whole
population.

169
00:09:22.019 --> 00:09:26.810
Dividing by n − 1 corrects the resulting
downward bias in variance.

170
00:09:26.890 --> 00:09:28.907
Here's an example.

171
00:09:28.987 --> 00:09:33.408
For farm B, the descriptive variance was 4:

172
00:09:33.488 --> 00:09:38.526
we divided the sum of squared deviations, 16,
by 4.

173
00:09:38.606 --> 00:09:42.843
To estimate population variance instead,

174
00:09:42.923 --> 00:09:47.239
we divide by the number of observations minus
one: 4 − 1, or 3.

175
00:09:47.331 --> 00:09:51.446
This gives a variance estimate of 5.33.

176
00:09:51.526 --> 00:09:56.068
It is larger than the variance used

177
00:09:56.148 --> 00:10:00.871
to describe the observed data, reflecting the
correction needed

178
00:10:00.951 --> 00:10:05.633
when estimating population variance from a
sample.

179
00:10:06.105 --> 00:10:10.366
Remember: divide by n to describe

180
00:10:10.446 --> 00:10:14.432
the observations in your sample; divide by n −
1

181
00:10:14.512 --> 00:10:19.293
when using an appropriate sample to estimate
population variance.

182
00:10:19.775 --> 00:10:22.151
Another question arises

183
00:10:22.231 --> 00:10:24.973
when interpreting standard deviation:

184
00:10:25.053 --> 00:10:28.579
relative to what value are we measuring
spread?

185
00:10:28.659 --> 00:10:30.826
Imagine two plant species.

186
00:10:30.906 --> 00:10:32.969
Species A has mean growth

187
00:10:33.049 --> 00:10:35.634
of 5 cm and a standard deviation of 1 cm.

188
00:10:35.714 --> 00:10:39.843
Species B has a mean of 20 cm and a standard
deviation of 2 cm.

189
00:10:39.935 --> 00:10:43.596
Looking only at standard deviations,

190
00:10:43.676 --> 00:10:47.337
B's is larger: 2 cm rather than 1 cm.

191
00:10:47.417 --> 00:10:52.324
But that's in absolute terms. In relative
terms, 2 cm

192
00:10:52.404 --> 00:10:57.437
compared with a mean of 20 is different from 1
cm compared with 5.

193
00:10:57.517 --> 00:11:01.507
Taking the ratio, 1 cm divided by 5 cm gives
20%.

194
00:11:01.599 --> 00:11:05.037
Meanwhile, 2 cm divided by 20 cm gives 10%.

195
00:11:05.117 --> 00:11:09.625
So the absolute spread is larger

196
00:11:09.705 --> 00:11:14.902
for B, but its relative spread is smaller than
A's.

197
00:11:14.982 --> 00:11:18.190
B's standard deviation is

198
00:11:18.270 --> 00:11:21.635
10% of its mean, whereas A's is 20%.

199
00:11:22.751 --> 00:11:25.072
What we've just calculated

200
00:11:25.152 --> 00:11:27.873
is called the coefficient of variation.

201
00:11:27.953 --> 00:11:30.923
It is the standard deviation divided by the
mean.

202
00:11:31.003 --> 00:11:34.124
As the definition suggests, it measures spread

203
00:11:34.204 --> 00:11:37.325
relative to the mean, rather than in absolute
units.

204
00:11:37.405 --> 00:11:39.876
This lets us compare different scales,

205
00:11:39.956 --> 00:11:43.107
or even measurements expressed in different
units.

206
00:11:43.615 --> 00:11:46.875
In our example, species A

207
00:11:46.955 --> 00:11:51.003
has greater relative variability, according to
this coefficient.

208
00:11:51.551 --> 00:11:54.315
Be careful when using the coefficient of
variation:

209
00:11:54.395 --> 00:11:57.573
it isn't appropriate for every measurement
scale.

210
00:11:57.653 --> 00:12:00.647
If the mean is close to zero,

211
00:12:00.727 --> 00:12:03.858
we're dividing by a very small number,

212
00:12:03.938 --> 00:12:07.024
so the coefficient can become extremely large.

213
00:12:07.104 --> 00:12:09.868
That can produce very unstable ratios.

214
00:12:09.948 --> 00:12:11.875
Watch out for this situation.

215
00:12:11.967 --> 00:12:15.289
Likewise, if values can be positive or
negative,

216
00:12:15.369 --> 00:12:19.147
the coefficient of variation becomes harder to
interpret.

217
00:12:19.227 --> 00:12:21.787
Finally, when extreme values are present,

218
00:12:21.867 --> 00:12:25.087
both the mean and standard deviation are
sensitive to them,

219
00:12:25.167 --> 00:12:27.119
whether they are unusually low or high.

220
00:12:27.199 --> 00:12:30.245
The coefficient of variation is therefore
sensitive to them too.

221
00:12:30.325 --> 00:12:33.371
It's a measure of relative spread

222
00:12:33.451 --> 00:12:36.549
that suits some situations, but not all.

223
00:12:36.629 --> 00:12:40.067
Used appropriately, it helps compare different
scales.

224
00:12:40.635 --> 00:12:44.175
Let's take a more concrete example

225
00:12:44.255 --> 00:12:47.846
of when the coefficient of variation is not
meaningful.

226
00:12:47.926 --> 00:12:50.461
Consider temperature

227
00:12:50.541 --> 00:12:53.378
in degrees Celsius. Zero degrees Celsius

228
00:12:53.458 --> 00:12:56.143
is a reference point, not an absence of
temperature.

229
00:12:56.223 --> 00:12:59.412
Changing that reference point when converting
scales

230
00:12:59.492 --> 00:13:02.630
changes the coefficient of variation, even
though

231
00:13:02.710 --> 00:13:06.079
the physical phenomenon, temperature, is
unchanged.

232
00:13:06.171 --> 00:13:09.656
So this measure isn't appropriate

233
00:13:09.736 --> 00:13:13.046
for the Celsius scale, because its zero

234
00:13:13.126 --> 00:13:16.983
doesn't represent an absence of the measured
quantity.

235
00:13:17.403 --> 00:13:20.298
To finish this session, we'll connect these
ideas

236
00:13:20.378 --> 00:13:24.879
to sampling and probability through the law of
large numbers.

237
00:13:24.959 --> 00:13:28.449
Imagine an agricultural cooperative estimating

238
00:13:28.529 --> 00:13:32.376
the mean mass of the seeds it is planting.

239
00:13:32.456 --> 00:13:36.184
It starts by selecting just five seeds,

240
00:13:36.264 --> 00:13:40.191
then gradually increases to 10, 30, 100 and
500,

241
00:13:40.539 --> 00:13:43.343
to see how the sample mean changes relative to

242
00:13:43.837 --> 00:13:46.033
the mean of all the seeds in the population.

243
00:13:46.517 --> 00:13:49.933
If the sampling is appropriate

244
00:13:50.013 --> 00:13:53.371
and the underlying distribution has a finite
mean,

245
00:13:53.451 --> 00:13:56.752
the sample mean tends towards the population
mean

246
00:13:56.832 --> 00:14:00.649
as the number of observations increases.

247
00:14:00.729 --> 00:14:04.718
At first, each new seed can shift the mean
substantially.

248
00:14:04.798 --> 00:14:09.589
As the sample grows, an additional observation

249
00:14:09.669 --> 00:14:14.425
generally has less influence on the sample
mean.

250
00:14:14.517 --> 00:14:17.487
The fluctuations become smaller,

251
00:14:17.567 --> 00:14:20.311
and the mean gradually settles down,

252
00:14:20.391 --> 00:14:23.723
here around the expected mean of 1 g.

253
00:14:24.197 --> 00:14:27.405
That's the intuition behind the law of large
numbers.

254
00:14:27.485 --> 00:14:32.305
A mean based on five observations is often
unstable.

255
00:14:32.385 --> 00:14:35.034
The next measurement can

256
00:14:35.114 --> 00:14:38.074
have a large effect on the overall mean.

257
00:14:38.154 --> 00:14:40.679
Adding a sixth observation

258
00:14:40.759 --> 00:14:44.233
gives that new value a weight of about 17%.

259
00:14:44.325 --> 00:14:47.246
Adding a 501st observation to the

260
00:14:47.326 --> 00:14:50.346
previous 500 generally changes the mean much
less.

261
00:14:50.426 --> 00:14:53.249
That's the idea of the law of large numbers:

262
00:14:53.329 --> 00:14:56.102
as the sample size grows, the mean

263
00:14:56.182 --> 00:14:59.104
becomes more stable and fluctuations become
smaller.

264
00:14:59.184 --> 00:15:02.695
It doesn't correct selection bias. As I
mentioned

265
00:15:02.775 --> 00:15:06.336
in the first video, if we select badly,

266
00:15:06.416 --> 00:15:10.057
for example by choosing only large seeds,

267
00:15:10.149 --> 00:15:14.469
the fluctuations may still become smaller

268
00:15:14.549 --> 00:15:19.220
over time, but the mean won't approach the
expected

269
00:15:19.300 --> 00:15:23.993
1 g. We're averaging only large seeds, which
creates bias.

270
00:15:24.469 --> 00:15:26.516
Our selection has changed

271
00:15:26.596 --> 00:15:29.266
the population we actually observe.

272
00:15:29.346 --> 00:15:32.639
We intended to study all the seeds,

273
00:15:32.719 --> 00:15:36.375
but our observed population consists only of
large seeds.

274
00:15:36.455 --> 00:15:39.073
Nor does increasing the sample size fix
calibration errors.

275
00:15:39.153 --> 00:15:41.927
Suppose we selected the seeds correctly,

276
00:15:42.007 --> 00:15:45.942
but our measuring instrument has a bias,

277
00:15:46.022 --> 00:15:51.103
systematically adding 0.2 g. Adding more
observations

278
00:15:51.183 --> 00:15:56.345
won't make the sample mean converge to the
correct mean of 1 g.

279
00:15:56.917 --> 00:15:59.541
Again, the law of large numbers tells us

280
00:15:59.621 --> 00:16:02.982
that increasing sample size reduces

281
00:16:03.062 --> 00:16:06.079
random fluctuations. It still doesn't correct

282
00:16:06.159 --> 00:16:09.372
biases introduced by a flawed procedure.

283
00:16:09.452 --> 00:16:13.010
It concerns the stability of the mean, not

284
00:16:13.090 --> 00:16:16.599
the shape of the distribution. Whether the
distribution is

285
00:16:16.679 --> 00:16:20.857
normal or not, symmetrical or asymmetrical,
with several groups

286
00:16:21.205 --> 00:16:24.662
or one large group, under the conditions
discussed,

287
00:16:24.742 --> 00:16:28.662
the law of large numbers says that the mean

288
00:16:28.742 --> 00:16:32.315
becomes more stable as the number of
observations

289
00:16:32.395 --> 00:16:36.337
increases. It doesn't determine the underlying
distribution's shape.

290
00:16:37.525 --> 00:16:42.035
Let's briefly recap what we've covered.

291
00:16:42.115 --> 00:16:46.769
We have several measures for describing a
distribution.

292
00:16:48.405 --> 00:16:50.654
The mean and median

293
00:16:50.734 --> 00:16:53.038
describe its centre.

294
00:16:53.118 --> 00:16:56.343
Variance and standard deviation describe its
spread,

295
00:16:56.423 --> 00:17:00.027
as does the interquartile range, which we'll
cover later.

296
00:17:00.107 --> 00:17:02.194
For relative spread,

297
00:17:02.274 --> 00:17:04.523
we can use the coefficient of variation.

298
00:17:04.603 --> 00:17:08.369
We need to check that it's appropriate in our
setting.

299
00:17:08.449 --> 00:17:12.025
It allows comparisons across different scales
and units

300
00:17:12.117 --> 00:17:14.563
because it has no units of its own.

301
00:17:15.055 --> 00:17:18.017
We can also use a histogram to visualise

302
00:17:18.097 --> 00:17:21.202
the distribution and examine its shape.

303
00:17:21.282 --> 00:17:24.054
It doesn't replace looking up an exact value

304
00:17:24.134 --> 00:17:27.096
in the table, but it gives us

305
00:17:27.176 --> 00:17:30.090
a broader view of the distribution.

306
00:17:30.170 --> 00:17:32.752
And that is much easier to grasp visually.

307
00:17:32.832 --> 00:17:35.319
Finally, the population we actually represent

308
00:17:35.399 --> 00:17:37.886
depends on the observation procedure we
choose.

309
00:17:37.966 --> 00:17:39.977
Think carefully beforehand about

310
00:17:40.057 --> 00:17:42.291
the biases your study might introduce.

311
00:17:42.639 --> 00:17:44.914
The key point is this:

312
00:17:44.994 --> 00:17:47.437
no single measure is enough on its own.

313
00:17:47.517 --> 00:17:50.578
Every measure gives incomplete information.

314
00:17:50.658 --> 00:17:53.774
By using several together,

315
00:17:53.854 --> 00:17:57.723
we can build a fuller understanding of our
data.
